gains. A cash-dealing mutual fund must sell holdings to meet redemptions, and those sales can generate capital gain distributions that are passed to all remaining shareholders, including those who did not sell.\n\n**Limits of the benefit:**\n- It applies only to in-kind redemptions. Cash creations or redemptions, and rebalancing or index-change sales, can still realize gains.\n- It depends on AP participation and creation/redemption activity. Some ETFs, such as many bond and international funds, rely more on cash.\n- Short-term gains are still distributed as ordinary income, and dividends and interest are unaffected.\n- Your own sale of ETF shares is still taxable, so the benefit is avoiding fund-level distributions, not eliminating tax.\n- The fund must qualify as a RIC, and the result is deferral of gains rather than permanent exemption.","ttft_ms":5443,"type":"result","duration_ms":7347,"uuid":"d34a39bc-cd00-4d17-b9b3-475bb08ee5c1","ttft_stream_ms":461,"time_to_request_ms":97,"first_content_frame_ms":461,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
